{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "255",
    "case_summary": "A woman in her 40s with a medical history of pulmonary embolism and deep venous thrombosis presented with a 6-month history of pruritic, pink scaling plaques on her face and neck. The lesions subsequently involved her chest, abdomen, and back and flared with sun exposure. Previous treatment with hydroxychloroquine sulfate, fluocinonide ointment, 0.05%, and tacrolimus ointment, 0.1% failed to improve the condition.\n\nWell-demarcated plaques with an overlying scale crust occurring in a photodistributed pattern on the central face, with involvement of chest, abdomen, and back.\n\n{'Laboratory Tests': ['Antinuclear antibody titers positive at 1:640', 'Lymphopenia present', 'Normocytic anemia present', 'Negative or within normal limits: antiribonucleoprotein, antihistone, antiphospholipid, anti-Smith, anti-Ro/Sjögren syndrome antigen A, and anti-La/Sjögren syndrome antigen B antibodies', 'Normal C3, C4, CH50, rapid plasma reagin, and creatinine levels', 'Normal erythrocyte sedimentation rate', 'Normal comprehensive metabolic profile', 'Normal urinalysis'], 'Histopathology': {'Biopsy Findings': 'Epidermal acanthosis, spongiosis, and acantholytic keratinocytes. Dense lymphoplasmacellular infiltrate in the dermis, with scattered neutrophils and eosinophils. Foci of eosinophilic spongiosis in the epidermis.', 'Direct Immunofluorescence': 'Granular and linear deposition of IgG and C3 at the basement membrane zone and abundant intercellular deposition of IgG and C3 in the upper two-thirds of the epidermis.'}, 'Images': {'Figure A': 'Shows well-demarcated plaques with an overlying scale crust occurring in a photodistributed pattern on the central face', 'Figure B': 'Hematoxylin-eosin staining, original magnification ×4', 'Figure C': 'Hematoxylin-eosin staining, original magnification ×10'}}",
